# Fall Detection System — Complete Project Walkthrough

A real-time fall detection system that uses **YOLOv8 object detection** to identify people in video frames and determine if they have fallen based on bounding-box geometry.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A["📹 fall.mp4"] --> B["📦 OpenCV\nRead & Resize"]
    B --> C["🧠 YOLOv8s\nObject Detection"]
    C --> D{"Person?\nconf > 80%?"}
    D -- Yes --> E{"height < width?"}
    E -- Yes --> F["🚨 Fall Detected"]
    E -- No --> G["✅ Standing"]
    D -- No --> H["⏭️ Skip"]
    F --> I["🖥️ Display Frame"]
    G --> I
```

---

## File-by-File Breakdown

---

### 1. [main.py](file:///e:/projects/Fall-Detection/main.py) — The Core Application

This is the **only code file** in the project. It runs the entire detection pipeline in ~55 lines.

#### Imports (Lines 1–4)

```python
import cv2          # OpenCV — video capture, frame manipulation, display
import cvzone       # OpenCV helper — pretty bounding boxes & text overlays
import math         # math.ceil() to round confidence percentages
from ultralytics import YOLO  # YOLOv8 model loader and inference engine
```

| Library | Why it's used |
|---|---|
| **cv2 (OpenCV)** | Industry-standard computer vision library. Handles reading video frames, resizing, drawing, and displaying the output window. |
| **cvzone** | A lightweight wrapper around OpenCV that provides `cornerRect()` for styled bounding boxes and `putTextRect()` for text with background — saves boilerplate code. |
| **math** | Only used for `math.ceil()` to convert float confidence (e.g. `0.873`) into a clean integer percentage (`87`). |
| **ultralytics (YOLO)** | The official YOLOv8 library by Ultralytics. Loads the pre-trained model, runs inference on each frame, and returns structured detection results. |

#### Video & Model Setup (Lines 6–12)

```python
cap = cv2.VideoCapture('fall.mp4')       # Open the test video
model = YOLO('yolov8s.pt')               # Load YOLOv8-small model

classnames = []
with open('classes.txt', 'r') as f:
    classnames = f.read().splitlines()   # Load 80 COCO class names into a list
```

| Variable | What it does |
|---|---|
| `cap` | An OpenCV `VideoCapture` object that reads [fall.mp4](file:///e:/projects/Fall-Detection/fall.mp4) frame-by-frame. Could also be replaced with `0` for a live webcam feed. |
| `model` | The loaded YOLOv8s neural network. The `'s'` stands for "small" — a balance between speed and accuracy. |
| `classnames` | A Python list of 80 object names (index 0 = `"person"`, index 1 = `"bicycle"`, etc.) used to convert numeric class IDs into human-readable labels. |

#### Main Detection Loop (Lines 15–50)

```python
while True:
    ret, frame = cap.read()                      # Step 1: Read next frame
    frame = cv2.resize(frame, (980, 740))         # Step 2: Resize to fixed dimensions
    results = model(frame)                        # Step 3: Run YOLOv8 inference
```

**Step-by-step for each frame:**

| Step | Code | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| **Read** | `cap.read()` | Grabs the next video frame. `ret` is `True` if successful, `False` at end of video. |
| **Resize** | `cv2.resize(frame, (980, 740))` | Standardizes frame size to 980×740 pixels regardless of source resolution. |
| **Detect** | `model(frame)` | Feeds the frame through the YOLOv8 neural network. Returns a list of detection results. |

#### Processing Each Detection (Lines 21–45)

```python
for info in results:                              # Loop over result batches
    parameters = info.boxes                       # Get all bounding boxes
    for box in parameters:                        # Loop over each detection
        x1, y1, x2, y2 = box.xyxy[0]             # Top-left & bottom-right corners
        x1, y1, x2, y2 = int(x1), int(y1), int(x2), int(y2)
        confidence = box.conf[0]                  # Detection confidence (0.0–1.0)
        class_detect = box.cls[0]                 # Class ID (integer)
        class_detect = int(class_detect)
        class_detect = classnames[class_detect]   # Convert ID → name ("person")
        conf = math.ceil(confidence * 100)        # Convert to percentage
```

**Key variables explained:**

| Variable | Example Value | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `x1, y1` | `120, 50` | Top-left corner of the bounding box (pixels) |
| `x2, y2` | `280, 400` | Bottom-right corner of the bounding box (pixels) |
| `confidence` | `0.92` | How sure the model is that this object exists (0–1) |
| `class_detect` | `"person"` | The name of the detected object class |
| `conf` | `92` | Confidence as a clean percentage |

#### The Fall Detection Logic (Lines 33–45)

```python
height = y2 - y1          # Bounding box height
width  = x2 - x1          # Bounding box width
threshold = height - width # The key heuristic

if conf > 80 and class_detect == 'person':
    # Draw bounding box + label for high-confidence person detections
    cvzone.cornerRect(frame, [x1, y1, width, height], l=30, rt=6)
    cvzone.putTextRect(frame, f'{class_detect}', [x1 + 8, y1 - 12], thickness=2, scale=2)

if threshold < 0:
    # If the person is wider than tall → Fall Detected
    cvzone.putTextRect(frame, 'Fall Detected', [height, width], thickness=2, scale=2)
```

**The core idea visualized:**

```
  Standing Person          Fallen Person
  ┌──────┐  height > width  ┌──────────────┐  width > height
  │      │                  │              │
  │      │                  └──────────────┘
  │      │
  │      │
  └──────┘
  threshold > 0  ✅          threshold < 0  🚨
```

- **Standing:** Bounding box is taller than wide → `height - width > 0` → Normal
- **Fallen:** Bounding box is wider than tall → `height - width < 0` → **FALL DETECTED**

#### Display & Exit (Lines 48–54)

```python
cv2.imshow('frame', frame)              # Show the annotated frame in a window
if cv2.waitKey(1) & 0xFF == ord('t'):   # Wait 1ms; if 't' is pressed, exit
    break

cap.release()                           # Release the video file handle
cv2.destroyAllWindows()                 # Close the display window
```

| Function | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `cv2.imshow()` | Renders the annotated frame in a GUI window titled "frame" |
| `cv2.waitKey(1)` | Pauses 1ms between frames (controls playback speed) and listens for keypresses |
| `ord('t')` | The ASCII code for `t` — pressing `t` exits the loop |
| `cap.release()` | Frees the video file so the OS can close it |
| `cv2.destroyAllWindows()` | Cleans up all OpenCV GUI windows |

---

### 2. [yolov8s.pt](file:///e:/projects/Fall-Detection/yolov8s.pt) — Pre-trained Model Weights (~22.5 MB)

This is a **binary file** containing the trained neural network parameters for **YOLOv8-small**.

| Property | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Architecture** | YOLOv8s (small variant) |
| **Training data** | COCO dataset (330K images, 80 object classes) |
| **What it detects** | People, cars, animals, furniture, electronics — all 80 COCO classes |
| **Why "small"** | The YOLOv8 family has 5 sizes: `n` (nano) → `s` (small) → `m` (medium) → `l` (large) → `x` (extra-large). The `s` variant balances speed and accuracy for real-time use. |
| **How it's used** | Loaded by `YOLO('yolov8s.pt')` which reconstructs the neural network architecture and fills it with these trained weights. |

> [!NOTE]
> The model detects **all 80 classes** but the code only acts on `"person"` detections. The other 79 classes are effectively ignored.

---

### 3. [classes.txt](file:///e:/projects/Fall-Detection/classes.txt) — COCO Class Labels (80 lines)

A plain text file with one class name per line, corresponding to the **COCO dataset's 80 object categories**.

```
Line 0:  person        ← This is the only class used for fall detection
Line 1:  bicycle
Line 2:  car
...
Line 79: toothbrush
```

| Purpose | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Why it exists** | YOLOv8 returns a numeric class ID (e.g., `0`). This file maps that ID to a readable name (e.g., `"person"`). |
| **How it's used** | Loaded into `classnames` list → `classnames[0]` returns `"person"`. |
| **Could it be removed?** | Technically yes — Ultralytics' YOLO model has a built-in `model.names` dictionary. But using an external file gives explicit control over label names. |

---

### 4. [fall.mp4](file:///e:/projects/Fall-Detection/fall.mp4) — Test Video (~1.4 MB)

A short video clip showing a person falling, used as the input source for detection.

| Property | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Purpose** | Demo/test input — provides frames for the detection pipeline |
| **Replaceable?** | Yes — change `cv2.VideoCapture('fall.mp4')` to `cv2.VideoCapture(0)` for **live webcam** input, or point to any other video file. |

